WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.760
For most of the last century, the rule was that the neurons you are born with are

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:08.760
the neurons you keep.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.060
Adult rodents break that rule.

4
00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.560
All their lives they make new neurons in two places.

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.040
One is a thin layer in the wall of the lateral ventricles, the subventricular zone.

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.800
Its young neurons travel forward to the olfactory bulb.

7
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.180
The other is the dentate gyrus of the hippocampus.

8
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.140
In both places, the new neurons join working circuits.

9
00:00:32.133 --> 00:00:33.353
Which cell makes them?

10
00:00:34.173 --> 00:00:38.593
In mice, a drug killed the fast-dividing cells of the subventricular zone.

11
00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:41.413
Cells that look like astrocytes survived.

12
00:00:42.273 --> 00:00:44.333
They began dividing and rebuilt the zone.

13
00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:46.913
So the stem cell is astrocyte-like.

14
00:00:47.753 --> 00:00:51.693
It makes fast-dividing precursors, which make young neurons that migrate.

15
00:00:52.573 --> 00:00:55.033
The dentate gyrus has stem cells like these too.

16
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:58.207
How can anyone tell that a neuron is new?

17
00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:00.807
Give a labelled DNA building block.

18
00:01:01.587 --> 00:01:03.927
Only cells copying their DNA build it in.

19
00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:08.787
A neuron that has stopped dividing normally does not copy its DNA again.

20
00:01:09.687 --> 00:01:14.447
So a labelled neuron found weeks later most likely came from a cell that divided

21
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:18.847
after the dose. A second stain shows that the labelled cell is a neuron.

22
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:22.650
A new neuron matters only if it is wired in.

23
00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.390
Over several weeks, new granule neurons grow dendrites and send axons into the

24
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:29.390
hippocampal circuit.

25
00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.910
In adult canaries, new neurons in a song nucleus were found to respond to sound.

26
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:36.973
What are they for?

27
00:01:37.853 --> 00:01:42.653
In mice, young dentate neurons are thought to help with pattern separation, telling

28
00:01:42.653 --> 00:01:44.573
apart similar places or events.

29
00:01:45.613 --> 00:01:49.913
Mice in which adult neurogenesis was blocked did worse at telling apart nearby

30
00:01:49.913 --> 00:01:54.733
locations. In the olfactory bulb, new interneurons have been linked to odour

31
00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.653
learning. Signals in the niche and experience set the output.

32
00:01:58.653 --> 00:02:02.313
Mice with a running wheel had more new dentate neurons.

33
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:04.467
What about people?

34
00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:11.607
Bomb tests in the 1950s and early 1960s put extra carbon-14 into the air, and into

35
00:02:11.607 --> 00:02:13.467
the DNA of cells made then.

36
00:02:14.407 --> 00:02:18.587
Neurons at the back of the cortex, and in the olfactory bulb, are as old as the

37
00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:21.767
person. Some hippocampal neurons are younger.

38
00:02:22.827 --> 00:02:26.907
Stains for young neurons disagree, partly because the stain fades after death.

39
00:02:27.687 --> 00:02:32.347
Recent reviews read the evidence as favouring some new neurons in the adult human

40
00:02:32.347 --> 00:02:34.407
hippocampus, from several methods.

41
00:02:35.307 --> 00:02:37.367
How many, and what they do, is still open.
